{
  "abstract": "A cumulative Möbius coefficient can cancel a principal quartic Gauss contribution without deleting its prime tail. We construct a smooth arithmetic cutoff from the odd Gaussian lattice and bound the complete Gaussian-row mean with squarefree columns and this prescribed coefficient. At inverse length $X=L^x$, its exponent is $5/4-2x$ on $[0,1/8]$, $1$ on $[1/8,1/3]$, $1/2+3x/2$ on $[1/3,3/8]$, $7/8+x/2$ on $[3/8,1/2]$, $3/4+3x/4$ on $[1/2,3/5]$, and $4/5+2x/3$ on $[3/5,27/40)$, up to an arbitrary small loss. This improves the generic exponent $5/4$ for fixed $0<x<27/40$. The principal argument cancels a reciprocal zeta factor in the whole exterior vector. Natural character zeros permit pointwise aggregation of forced divisors; a powerful-part expansion controls the maximum over inverse lengths. De Faveri's sieve, the natural fourth moment of Paper105/v2, and a short-product estimate proved from the quadratic large sieve give two mixed bounds. A third uses buffered reciprocal bounds and the density estimates of Papers104/v2 and105/v2, after verifying that the actual dyadic twists have order dividing four. Fourth-power towers and small frequency blocks are restored explicitly. A rectangular theorem and one exact detector cutoff with logarithmic gap loss are included. Exact finite controls accompany the author proof; independent review is pending. At $X=L^{3/4}$ the mixed exponent improves to $13/10$, still above $5/4$. The ordinary detector has a different column Gauss phase; its direct mixed-moment consumer yields no new density bound. No zero-free or RH theorem is claimed.",
